    ncertSummary: "The 19th century witnessed profound economic, political, social, and technological transformations. Economists identify three distinct 'flows' or movements within international economic exchanges:\n1. The Flow of Trade: Primarily trade in goods such as wheat, raw cotton, and manufactured textiles.\n2. The Flow of Labour: The migration of millions of people in search of employment across oceans.\n3. The Flow of Capital: The movement of short-term and long-term financial investments across vast distances for agricultural and industrial development.\n\nA classic case study of this emerging global economy is the abolition of the British Corn Laws. Population growth from the late 18th century increased the demand for food grains in Britain. Under pressure from landed landed-gentry groups, the government restricted the import of corn by imposing heavy import tariffs—laws commonly known as the 'Corn Laws'. Unhappy with soaring food prices, urban industrialists and town dwellers forced the British parliament to abolish the Corn Laws.\n\nThe abolition of the Corn Laws dramatically restructured the world economy:\n- Food could now be imported into Britain far more cheaply than it could be grown domestically.\n- British agriculture was unable to compete with cheap overseas imports. Vast areas of uncultivated land fell out of cultivation, and thousands of men and women were thrown out of agricultural work, flocking to cities or migrating overseas.\n- Cheaper food in Britain led to surging consumption. To satisfy British appetite, lands across Russia, Eastern Europe, North America, and Australia were rapidly cleared to expand commercial wheat and food production.\n- Building agricultural infrastructure required capital and labor: railways were laid to connect farms to harbors, new ports were built, and vast labor migration took place. Nearly 50 million people emigrated from Europe to America and Australia in the 19th century.",
