    ncertSummary: "The First World War (1914–1918) was the first modern industrial war. It witnessed machine guns, tanks, aircraft, and chemical weapons on an unimaginable scale. Over 9 million men were killed and 20 million injured. The war broke international economic ties, transformed the US from an international debtor to the world's supreme creditor nation, and sowed the seeds of structural global economic instability.\n\nThe Great Depression began around 1929 and lasted until the late 1930s. During this catastrophic period, most parts of the world experienced catastrophic declines in production, employment, incomes, and trade. Agricultural regions and communities were the worst affected because the collapse of agricultural prices was prolonged and steeper than industrial prices.\n\nTwo Primary Causes of the Great Depression:\n1. Agricultural Overproduction: Agricultural production expanded during and after WWI. As agricultural prices fell, farmers tried to expand production to maintain their overall volume of income. This glutted the market with unsold grain, pushing prices down even lower. Farm produce rotted in storage while farmers went bankrupt.\n2. Withdrawal of US Loans: In the mid-1920s, many European nations financed their investments through US loans. When the US economy began to wobble in 1928, US overseas lenders panicked. In the first half of 1928, US loans abroad stood at over $1 billion; a year later it collapsed to one-quarter of that amount. Countries dependent on US financing faced total financial ruin. In 1929, the New York Wall Street stock exchange crashed.\n\nImpact of the Great Depression on India:\nThe Depression immediately devastated Indian colonial trade. India's exports and imports nearly halved between 1928 and 1934. As agricultural prices plunged by 50 percent, Indian peasants and farmers suffered far worse than urban dwellers. Although agricultural prices crashed, the British colonial government ruthlessly refused to reduce land revenue demands. Peasants had to sell their accumulated gold jewelry and precious heirlooms to pay revenue and survive. India became an exporter of precious metal (gold)—an outflow that famously financed Britain's financial recovery but left Indian villages plunged into generational debt.",
