    ncertSection: "NCERT History Chapter 3, Section 4 & 4.3 (Pages 73–76)",
    ncertSummary: "The Second World War (1939–1945) caused immense devastation across Europe and Asia, killing at least 60 million people and destroying vast industrial infrastructure. Two crucial post-war realities emerged: the rise of the United States as the dominant economic, military, and diplomatic power, and the emergence of the Soviet Union as a communist superpower.\n\nPost-War International Economic Settlement: The Bretton Woods Conference:\nIn July 1944, delegates from 44 Allied nations met at Bretton Woods, New Hampshire (USA) to agree on a framework for post-war economic stability and full employment. The conference established two sister institutions—known as the 'Bretton Woods Twins':\n1. International Monetary Fund (IMF): Established to manage external surpluses and deficits of member states and oversee international financial stability.\n2. International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (World Bank): Set up to finance post-war reconstruction of war-shattered European economies.\n\nThe Bretton Woods system was based on fixed exchange rates. Currencies were pegged to the US dollar at a fixed rate, and the US dollar itself was anchored to gold at a fixed price of $35 per ounce of gold. The US held effective veto power over key IMF and World Bank decisions.\n\nDecolonisation, The Group of 77 (G-77) & NIEO:\nIn the decades following 1945, dozens of colonies in Asia and Africa gained political independence. However, their economies were crippled by centuries of colonial exploitation. The IMF and World Bank were designed originally to meet the needs of industrial Western countries and were ill-equipped to address poverty in developing nations.\n\nTo protect their interests against Western economic domination, developing countries organized themselves into the Group of 77 (G-77) to demand a New International Economic Order (NIEO). By the NIEO, they demanded:\n- Real control over their own natural resources.\n- Fairer prices for raw materials and commodities exported to the West.\n- Better access for manufactured goods into developed Western markets.\n- Genuine development assistance without imperialist political strings attached.\n\nBy the late 1960s, the US dollar lost its financial pre-eminence due to rising overseas military costs. Fixed exchange rates collapsed, replaced by floating exchange rates. Developing countries were forced to borrow from private Western commercial banks, leading to debt crises and structural adjustment programs in the 1980s.",
