    ncertSummary: "For centuries, silk and spices from China flowed into Europe along the Silk Route. In the 11th century, Chinese paper reached Europe via the same conduit. Paper made possible the production of manuscripts, carefully handwritten by scribes. Then, in 1295, Marco Polo, a great explorer, returned to Italy after many years of exploration in China. Marco Polo brought back the knowledge of woodblock printing. Italians began producing books with woodblocks, and soon the technology spread across Europe. Luxury editions were still handwritten on expensive vellum (animal skin parchment) for aristocratic circles, who viewed paper print as vulgar, while cheaper paper books were eagerly bought by students and merchants.\n\nAs the demand for books soared across Europe, hand copying could no longer satisfy the reading appetite. Scribes were slow, expensive, and prone to copying errors; manuscripts were fragile and clumsy to handle. Woodblock printing became increasingly popular, but Europe desperately needed a faster, mechanical printing mechanism.\n\nThe breakthrough occurred at Strasbourg, Germany, where Johann Gutenberg developed the first-known mechanical printing press in the 1430s–1448. Gutenberg was the son of a merchant who grew up on a large agricultural estate. He saw wine and olive presses, mastered the art of polishing stones, became an expert goldsmith, and acquired the expertise to create lead molds for making trinkets. Drawing upon this diverse technical knowledge, Gutenberg adapted existing technology: the olive press provided the mechanical model for the printing press, and metal molds were cast for lead alloy alphabetic movable letters. By 1448, Gutenberg perfected the system. The first book he printed was the Gutenberg Bible. Around 180 copies were printed over three years—an astonishing speed for that era! These copies were printed on paper and vellum, with empty border spaces hand-illuminated by artists for elite buyers.",
