    ncertSummary: "The shift from hand printing to mechanical printing was not just a technical development. It transformed the lives of people, changing their relationship to information and knowledge, and with institutions and authorities. It opened up new ways of looking at things. This transformation is known as the 'Print Revolution'.\n\nA New Reading Public Emerged:\nWith the printing press, a new reading public emerged. Printing reduced the cost of books. The time and labor required to produce each book dropped dramatically, and multiple copies could be produced with great ease. Books flooded the market, reaching out to an ever-growing readership.\n\nBefore the print revolution, reading was restricted to the elites. Common people lived in a world of oral culture: they heard sacred texts read out, ballads recited, and folk tales narrated. Knowledge was transferred orally. Books were expensive and scarce luxury commodities. Print created a wide reading public.\n\nBridging the Literacy Gap: Oral Culture Meets Print:\nHowever, the transition was not straightforward. Literacy rates in most European countries were extremely low until the late 17th century (often 10–15%). How could printers sell books to people who could not read? Printers began publishing popular ballads and folk tales illustrated with vivid woodcuts. These books were then sung and recited at village gatherings and in urban taverns. Thus, oral culture entered print, and printed material was orally transmitted. The line separating the hearing public and the reading public blurred, creating a collective audience for shared ideas.",
