    ncertSummary: "Print created the possibility of wide circulation of ideas, and introduced a new world of debate and discussion. Even those who disagreed with established authorities could now print and circulate their ideas. Through the printed message, they could persuade people to think differently, and move them to action.\n\nMartin Luther and the Protestant Reformation (1517):\nIn 1517, the religious reformer Martin Luther wrote 'Ninety-Five Theses', sharply criticizing many practices and rituals of the Roman Catholic Church, particularly the corrupt sale of indulgences. A printed copy of this was nailed to a church door in Wittenberg. It challenged the Church to debate his ideas. Luther's writings were immediately reproduced in vast quantities and read widely across Germany. Within weeks, Luther's translation of the New Testament sold 5,000 copies, and a second edition appeared in three months. Deeply moved by the transformative power of print, Luther famously proclaimed: 'Printing is the ultimate gift of God and the greatest one.' Print catalyzed the Protestant Reformation, permanently shattering the monopoly of the Roman Catholic Church.\n\nThe Fear of Print & Menocchio's Trial:\nNot everyone welcomed the printed book. Monarchs, conservative aristocrats, and the Catholic clergy were terrified of the consequences. They feared that if there was no control over what was printed and read, rebellious and irreligious thoughts would spread, subverting the authority of religion and monarchy.\n\nA famous example was Menocchio, a miller in northern Italy. In the 16th century, Menocchio began reading books available in his locality. He reinterpreted the message of the Bible and formulated a view of God and Creation that infuriated the Roman Catholic Church. When the Roman Church began its harsh Inquisition to crush heretical beliefs, Menocchio was hauled up twice, tortured, and ultimately executed. Terrified by such unrestrained popular reinterpretations of faith, the Roman Catholic Church imposed strict controls over publishers and booksellers, and from 1558 maintained the infamous 'Index of Prohibited Books' (Index Librorum Prohibitorum).",
