    ncertSummary: "Through the 17th and 18th centuries, literacy rates soared across Europe. Churches of various denominations set up schools in villages, carrying literacy to peasants and artisans. By the end of the 18th century, literacy in parts of Europe reached 60 to 80 percent. As literacy and schools spread, there was a virtual 'reading mania'. People wanted books to read, and printers produced books in ever-increasing numbers.\n\nNew forms of popular literature targeted common readers:\n- In England, penny chapbooks were sold by peddlers known as chapmen for a single penny, allowing even poor laborers to buy them.\n- In France, the 'Bibliothèque Bleue' were low-priced small books printed on poor-quality paper and bound in cheap blue covers.\n- Romances, historical tales, almanacs, and folktales were printed in millions.\n- Periodical press developed, combining news about trade and wars with entertainment.\n- Ideas of scientists and philosophers became accessible to the common masses. Maps and scientific diagrams were widely printed. When Isaac Newton published his discoveries, and thinkers like Voltaire, Jean-Jacques Rousseau, and Montesquieu published their critiques of despotism, their ideas reached a vast public.\n\nDid Print Cause the French Revolution (1789)?\nMany historians argue that print culture created the conditions within which the French Revolution occurred. Three compelling arguments are advanced:\n1. Popularised the Ideas of Enlightenment Thinkers: Writings of Voltaire and Rousseau attacked the sacred authority of the Church and the despotic power of monarchs. They argued for the rule of reason and critical judgment rather than custom and tradition.\n2. Created a Culture of Dialogue and Debate: All values, norms, and institutions were re-evaluated and discussed by a public that had become aware of the power of reason and questioning.\n3. Satirised Royalty and Exposed Morality: By the 1780s, an outpouring of literature, cartoons, and caricatures mocked the royalty's lavish lifestyles while common people suffered devastating famine. They painted monarchs as engrossed in sensual pleasures, raising public outrage against the monarchy.\n\nHowever, historians note that while print opened up ideas, people did not read just one type of literature. If they read Voltaire and Rousseau, they also read Church propaganda. Print did not directly dictate their minds, but it opened up the possibility of thinking differently.",
