    ncertSummary: "The 19th century saw vast leaps in mass literacy in Europe, bringing large numbers of children, women, and workers to print culture.\n\n1. Children as Readers:\nAs primary education became compulsory from the late 19th century, children became an important category of readers. Publishing for school textbooks became critical for printing houses. In 1857, a dedicated children's press was established in France. In Germany, the Grimm Brothers spent years compiling traditional folk tales from peasants, publishing their famous fairy-tale collection in 1812. Crucially, the Grimm brothers edited out anything they considered unsuitable for children or vulgar, creating sanitized moral stories.\n\n2. Women as Readers and Writers:\nWomen emerged as critical readers and authors. Penny magazines were published especially for women, containing manuals on domestic behavior, cooking, and housekeeping. When novels emerged in the 19th century, women were avid readers. Some of the greatest novelists were women: Jane Austen, the Brontë sisters (Charlotte, Emily, Anne), and George Eliot (Mary Ann Evans). Their writings defined a new type of woman: a person with willpower, strength of personality, determination, and the power to think critically.\n\n3. Workers & Lending Libraries:\nLending libraries had been around since the 17th century. In the 19th century, they became instruments for educating white-collar workers, artisans, and working-class people. When the working day was gradually shortened from the mid-19th century, workers had leisure time for self-improvement. Self-educated working-class authors wrote political tracts and autobiographies asserting their rights.\n\nTechnological Innovations in 19th-Century Printing:\nBy the mid-19th century, Richard M. Hoe of New York perfected the power-driven cylindrical rotary printing press. Capable of printing 8,000 sheets per hour, this was especially useful for mass-circulation newspapers. In the late 19th century, the offset press was developed, which could print in up to six colors at once. In the 1920s in England, cheap popular series were sold in bookstalls. During the 1930s Great Depression, publishers brought out cheap paperback editions (such as Penguin Books) to sustain sales amid economic hardship.",
