    ncertSection: "NCERT History Chapter 5, Section 6 & 6.1 (Pages 121–123)",
    ncertSummary: "India had a very rich and ancient tradition of handwritten manuscripts—in Sanskrit, Arabic, Persian, as well as in various vernacular languages. Manuscripts were copied on palm leaves or on handmade paper. Pages were beautifully illustrated with miniature paintings. They would either be pressed between wooden covers or sewn together to ensure preservation. Manuscripts continued to be produced well into the late 19th century.\n\nLimitations of Indian Manuscripts:\nManuscripts were highly expensive and fragile. They had to be handled with extreme care, and could not be read easily as the script was written in different styles. In pre-colonial Bengal, rural primary schools taught students how to write rather than read. Teachers dictated portions of texts from memory and students wrote them down. Many people became literate without ever reading a printed text.\n\nPrint Comes to India: The Portuguese Jesuit Press:\nThe printing press first came to Goa with Portuguese Jesuit missionaries in the mid-16th century. Jesuit priests learned Konkani and printed several tracts. By 1674, about 50 books had been printed in Konkani and Kanara languages. Catholic priests printed the first Tamil book in 1579 at Cochin, and in 1713 the first Malayalam book was printed by them. By 1710, Dutch Protestant missionaries had printed 32 Tamil texts, many of them translations of older works.\n\nJames Augustus Hickey & The Bengal Gazette (1780):\nThe English East India Company began importing presses in the late 17th century, but English printing blossomed only in the late 18th century. From 1780, James Augustus Hickey began to edit the 'Bengal Gazette', a weekly magazine that described itself as 'a commercial paper open to all, but influenced by none'. It was private English enterprise, independent of colonial control. Hickey published lots of advertisements, including those about import and sale of slaves. But he also published scurrilous gossip about senior East India Company officials in India. Enraged, Governor-General Warren Hastings persecuted Hickey and encouraged officially sanctioned newspapers to protect the colonial image. By the close of the 18th century, a number of newspapers appeared. The first weekly newspaper published by an Indian was Gangadhar Bhattacharya's 'Bengal Gazette', closely associated with Rammohun Roy.",
