    ncertSection: "NCERT History Chapter 5, Section 7 (Pages 123–125)",
    ncertSummary: "From the early 19th century, intense debates centered around religious matters. Different groups confronted the changes taking place within colonial society in different ways, offering a variety of new interpretations of the beliefs of different religions. Some criticized existing practices and campaigned for reform, while others fiercely defended the established orthodoxy. These debates were carried out in public and in print. Printed tracts and newspapers not only spread the new ideas, but they shaped the nature of the debate: a wider public could now participate in these public discussions and express their views. New ideas emerged through these clashes of opinions.\n\nHindu Social Reform Debates in Bengal:\nThis was a time of intense controversies between social and religious reformers and the Hindu orthodoxy over matters like sati, widow remarriage, monotheism, idolatry, and Brahmanical priesthood. In Bengal, as the debate intensified, tracts and newspapers proliferated, circulating arguments in the vernacular. To reach a wider audience, Raja Rammohun Roy published the 'Sambad Kaumudi' from 1821, advocating social reform and the abolition of Sati. The Hindu orthodox establishment commissioned the 'Samachar Chandrika' to oppose Rammohun's opinions. From 1822, two Persian newspapers were published: 'Jam-i-Jahan Numa' and 'Shamsul Akhbar'. In the same year, a Gujarati newspaper, the 'Bombay Samachar', made its appearance.\n\nMuslim Debates & The Deoband Seminary:\nIn north India, the Muslim ulema were deeply anxious about the collapse of Muslim royal dynasties. They feared that colonial rulers would encourage conversion, change Muslim personal laws, and undermine Islamic faith. To counter this, they used cheap lithographic presses, published Persian and Urdu translations of holy scriptures, and printed religious newspapers and tracts. The Deoband Seminary, founded in 1867, published thousands of fatwas telling Muslim readers how to conduct themselves in everyday life, and explaining Islamic doctrines. All through the 19th century, a number of Muslim sects and seminaries appeared, each using print to propagate their doctrine and counter rival interpretations.\n\nMass Vernacular Printing of Hindu Epics:\nAmong Hindus, print encouraged the reading of religious texts, especially in vernacular languages. The first printed edition of Tulsidas's 'Ramcharitmanas' came out from Calcutta in 1810. By the mid-19th century, cheap lithographic editions flooded north Indian markets. From the 1880s, the Naval Kishore Press at Lucknow and the Shri Venkateshwar Press in Bombay published numerous religious texts in vernaculars. In their printed and portable form, these texts could be read easily by the faithful at any place and time, or read out aloud to large groups of illiterate men and women. Religious texts thus reached a very wide circle of people, encouraging discussions, debates, and controversies within and among different religions.",
