    ncertSummary: "Before 1798, the colonial state under the East India Company was not too concerned with censorship. Strangely, its early measures to control printed matter were directed against Englishmen in India who were critical of Company misrule and worried that such criticisms might be used by its critics in England to attack the Company's trade monopoly.\n\nShift to Strict Censorship after the Revolt of 1857:\nBy the 1820s, the Calcutta Supreme Court passed regulations to control press freedom, and Governor-General Bentinck agreed to revise press laws in 1835. Thomas Macaulay formulated new rules that restored press freedoms. But after the Revolt of 1857, the attitude to freedom of the press changed completely. Enraged Englishmen demanded a clampdown on the 'native' press. As vernacular newspapers became assertively nationalist, they reported on colonial misrule and encouraged national identity.\n\nThe Vernacular Press Act (1878):\nIn 1878, the Vernacular Press Act was passed, modeled directly on the repressive Irish Press Laws. It provided the British colonial government with extensive rights to censor reports and editorials in the vernacular press:\n- The government kept regular track of vernacular newspapers published in different provinces.\n- When a report was judged as seditious, the newspaper was issued a formal warning.\n- If the warning was ignored, the press was liable to be seized and its printing machinery confiscated.\n\nDespite repressive measures, nationalist newspapers grew in numbers in all parts of India. They reported on colonial misgovernance and encouraged nationalist activities. When Punjab revolutionaries were deported in 1907, Bal Gangadhar Tilak wrote with great sympathy about them in his Marathi newspaper 'Kesari'. This led to his imprisonment in 1908, provoking widespread protests and strikes all across India. Print had become an unstoppable instrument of national liberation.",
